body:
## Problem

The model pool validator judges a `budgets` key only against the route's **direct** targets, but OpenCode reads budgets only by **concrete leaf model** (`provider/model`), resolving through any nested routes on the trail. With a route-reference target (`opencode-route/<id>`) in play, the validator is wrong in both directions:

1. **Dead key accepted:** a budget keyed by the route reference itself (`"opencode-route/child"`) validates and saves, but OpenCode never looks a budget up by that key — it only ever looks up the concrete leaf key — so the allowance silently never applies.
2. **Live key refused:** a budget keyed by a concrete model that the route reaches only through a referenced route is refused as "matches no target in the route", even though OpenCode would apply exactly that budget — its expansion collects budgets from every route on the trail by leaf key ("Budgets belong to the target: the outermost route that declares one wins").

This is the same saved-and-silently-ignored / refused-but-would-apply class as #347, #354 and #358, applied to the one key shape those fixes did not reach: budgets judged through route references (route references themselves were added to the validator in #356).

## Evidence

Validator, `packages/web/server/lib/routing/model-routes.js` on current main (`b76ea1689`):

- `validateBudgets` (line ~96) accepts a key when `targets.some((candidate) => keyNamesTarget(target, candidate))` over the route's direct targets; `keyNamesTarget` treats `opencode-route/child` as an ordinary reference, so a key equal to it matches.
- `validateModelRoute` calls `validateBudgets(id, route.budgets, route.targets)` with direct targets only; the cross-route knowledge added in `validateModelRoutes` for #356 is not used for budgets.

Direct probe against the real validator on current main:

```js
import { validateModelRoutes } from './packages/web/server/lib/routing/model-routes.js';

// (1) budget keyed by the route reference — accepted, never applies
validateModelRoutes({
  child:  { targets: ['openai/gpt-6-luna'] },
  parent: { targets: ['opencode-route/child'],
            budgets: { 'opencode-route/child': { requestsPerDay: 10 } } },
});
// → null (accepted)

// (2) budget keyed by the concrete leaf reached through the reference — refused, would apply
validateModelRoutes({
  child:  { targets: ['openai/gpt-6-luna'] },
  parent: { targets: ['opencode-route/child'],
            budgets: { 'openai/gpt-6-luna': { requestsPerDay: 10 } } },
});
// → 'route "parent" budget for "openai/gpt-6-luna" matches no target in the route'

// control: direct concrete target + same budget key → null (accepted, correct)
// control: weights keyed by 'opencode-route/child' → null (accepted, and correct — see below)
```

OpenCode side (developing-today/opencode, `packages/core/src/config/plugin/model-routes.ts`, `expand()`):

- Budget lookup is only ever by concrete leaf key: `const leafKey = `${resolved.providerID}/${resolved.id}`` and `routes.get(id)?.budgets?.[leafKey]`, searched over every route on the trail (`[...nextTrail].filter(usesRules).map(...)`), with the comment "Budgets belong to the target: the outermost route that declares one wins, wherever the target was first reached." No lookup ever uses an `opencode-route/<id>` key, so arm (1) can never apply; arm (2) is exactly the documented lookup, so it does apply (for a trail route at `rules` autonomy or with autonomy unset, per #359's `usesRules` reading).
- **Weights are different, and must stay accepted:** in the same `expand()`, a nested route is attached with key `` `${ModelRoute.PROVIDER_ID}/${ref.id}` `` and its weight is read as `current.weights?.[key]`, so a weights key of `opencode-route/<id>` is live configuration. The schema agrees (`packages/schema/src/config/model-routes.ts`, weights description: 'A nested route is keyed as "opencode-route/<id>"'; the budgets description says only "keyed by target model reference"). This asymmetry is budgets-specific — do not "fix" weights the same way.

## Why it matters

Budgets are the pool's spend guardrails. Arm (1) lets a user cap a nested pool as a unit — a natural thing to try, since weights genuinely work that way — and saves it as a silent no-op. Arm (2) blocks the documented way to cap a concrete model inside a nested pool from the parent route, forcing the budget onto the child route even when the parent is where the operator manages it. Both are exactly the misjudgements this validator exists to prevent.

## Acceptance criteria

- `validateModelRoutes` refuses a `budgets` key of the form `opencode-route/<id>` with a message naming the route and key and saying OpenCode reads budgets by concrete model reference, so the key would never apply.
- A `budgets` key naming a concrete model reachable from the route through its route-reference targets (transitively, cycle-safe, reusing the expansion reachability the #356 fix already computes) validates — judged at the set level in `validateModelRoutes`, since a single route cannot know the referenced routes' targets.
- Existing behaviour is unchanged for direct targets: exact and pattern-covered concrete keys still validate, pattern keys (#354) and no-target keys (#347) are still refused, and weights keyed by `opencode-route/<id>` still validate.
- Regression tests in `packages/web/server/lib/routing/model-routes.test.js` cover both arms plus the weights control, and the existing routing tests still pass.

Single-repo: the validator exists only in developing-today/openchamber (no copy in code/opencode/nix). Filed-only: the fix needs set-level budget judging in `validateModelRoutes`, a design step beyond a one-spot change.

Found by the new-issue-creator sweep, 2026-10-23.

## Problem

The model pool validator judges a route's schedule `targets` entries only against the route's **direct** targets, but OpenCode applies a route's schedules to the **concrete leaf models** the route reaches — including leaves reached through nested route references (`opencode-route/<id>`). With a route-reference target in play, the validator is wrong in both directions:

1. **Live entry refused:** a schedule entry naming a concrete model (or a pattern covering one) that the route reaches only through a referenced route is refused as "matches no target in the route", even though OpenCode would apply it — a route's schedule rules are inherited into nested groups and matched against each leaf's `provider/model` reference.
2. **Dead entry accepted:** a schedule entry naming the route reference itself (`opencode-route/<id>`) validates and saves, but OpenCode's schedule matcher only ever tests entries against concrete leaf references, so the entry can never match a leaf. (OpenCode's `ScheduleMatch` annotation claims targets "matches a nested route as `opencode-route/<id>`", but the only runtime matching site tests leaf refs — see Evidence; if a unit-matching path exists somewhere else, pin it down and keep this arm accepted instead.)

This is the schedules counterpart of #363 (budgets through route references, already filed) and the same saved-and-silently-ignored / refused-but-would-apply class as #347, #354 and #358. Route references were added to the validator by PR #361 (#356), which judged references at the set level in `validateModelRoutes` but left schedule judging in the single-route validator comparing against direct targets only; PR #362 then reworked that same schedule-matching code (variant refusal, case-insensitive judging) without extending it across references.

## Evidence

Validator, `packages/web/server/lib/routing/model-routes.js` on current main (`b76ea1689`):

- `validateSchedules` (line 218) receives only the route's own `targets` (called from `validateModelRoute`, line 357) and judges each entry with `targets.some(...)` over those direct targets (lines 243–251).
- The set-level reachability PR #361 added in `validateModelRoutes` (the `expanding` set, lines 385–399) is used only for missing-reference and expands-to-no-models refusals — schedule entries are never judged against the expanded leaf set.

Direct probe against the real validator on current main (`validateModelRoutes`):

```js
// (1) schedule entry naming the concrete leaf reached through a reference — refused, would apply
validateModelRoutes({
  a: { targets: ["opencode-route/b"], schedules: [{ when: {}, factor: 2, targets: ["openai/gpt-x"] }] },
  b: { targets: ["openai/gpt-x"] },
});
// → 'route "a" schedule 1 target "openai/gpt-x" matches no target in the route'
// same result for the pattern form: targets: ["openai/*"]

// (2) schedule entry naming the route reference itself — accepted, never matches a leaf
validateModelRoutes({
  a: { targets: ["opencode-route/b"], schedules: [{ when: {}, factor: 2, targets: ["opencode-route/b"] }] },
  b: { targets: ["openai/gpt-x"] },
});
// → null (accepted)

// controls: direct concrete target + same entry → null (accepted, correct)
// weights keyed by a leaf through a reference → refused, and CORRECT:
// OpenCode attaches weights per direct child (leaf key, or opencode-route/<id>
// for a nested route — see #363), so weights must not be "fixed" the same way.
```

OpenCode side (developing-today/opencode):

- `packages/core/src/config/plugin/model-routes.ts`: `routeWeightRules` (line 165) turns a route's `schedules` into weight rules carried on that route's node (line 245); expansion nests referenced routes as child nodes (line 257).
- `packages/core/src/model-route.ts`: when a nested group is walked, the parent's rules are inherited — `const rules = [...inherited, ...(node.schedules ?? [])]` (lines 845 and 862) — and `ruleMatches` (line 735) tests `match.targets` patterns against the leaf's `` `${target.providerID}/${target.id}` `` reference (line ~739). So a parent route's schedule entry naming a nested leaf applies to that leaf (arm 1), and no code path ever tests a schedule entry against an `opencode-route/<id>` label (arm 2) — the `ScheduleMatch` annotation at line 36 ("matches a nested route as `opencode-route/<id>`") is not borne out by the matcher.

## Why it matters

Schedules are how operators shift pool weights at peak hours. Arm (1) blocks a working schedule on any pool that composes other pools — the exact pools route references exist for — forcing the schedule onto the child route even when the parent is where it is managed. Arm (2) lets a user aim a schedule at a nested pool as a unit — a natural thing to try, since weights genuinely work that way — and saves it as a silent no-op, so the expected peak-hour weight change never happens. Both are exactly the misjudgements this validator exists to prevent.

## Acceptance criteria

- A schedule entry naming a concrete model (or a pattern that covers one, including partly overlapping pattern pairs, judged with PR #362's case-insensitive and variant rules) that the route reaches transitively through its route-reference targets validates — judged at the set level in `validateModelRoutes`, reusing the cycle-safe reachability the #356 fix already computes, since a single route cannot know the referenced routes' targets.
- Schedule entries that name no reachable leaf are still refused, including case-differing non-matches (PR #362 behaviour preserved for direct targets).
- Arm (2) is pinned down with runtime evidence from OpenCode: if no unit-matching path exists, `validateModelRoutes` refuses a schedule entry of the form `opencode-route/<id>` with a message naming the route and entry and saying OpenCode matches schedule targets against concrete `provider/model` references; if a unit-matching path does exist, a regression test demonstrates it and this arm is closed as not-a-bug.
- Existing behaviour is unchanged for direct targets, and weights keyed by `opencode-route/<id>` still validate (weights attach per direct child; do not change weights judging).
- Regression tests in `packages/web/server/lib/routing/model-routes.test.js` cover both arms plus the weights control, and the existing routing tests still pass.
- Coordinate with #363: budgets and schedules both need set-level judging against expanded targets in `validateModelRoutes`; one shared expansion helper should serve both rather than two parallel implementations.

Single-repo: the validator exists only in developing-today/openchamber (verified: no `validateSchedules`/`model-routes.js` counterpart in developing-today/code, developing-today/opencode, or dezren39/nix — OpenCode is the runtime whose behaviour is cited, not a place a fix is needed).

Found by the pr-diff-review sweep reviewing PRs #360, #361 and #362, 2026-10-23.

## Problem

The model pool validator accepts empty-string variant settings on a target — `defaultVariant: ""`, a `variants` remap with an empty value (`{ "low": "" }`), and a `variants` remap with an empty key (`{ "": "high" }`) — and saves them, but OpenCode silently drops every one of them, so the target behaves as if the setting was never written.

This is the same saved-and-silently-ignored class as #347 / #349 / #354, applied to the target variant fields, which the validator currently checks only for string type.

## Evidence

Validator, `packages/web/server/lib/routing/model-routes.js` on current main (`94c06ab4e`), `validateTarget` (lines 270–291):

- `defaultVariant` is refused only when it is not a string: `typeof target.defaultVariant !== "string"`.
- `variants` entries are refused only when a value is not a string; keys are not checked at all.

Direct probe against the real validator on current main:

```js
import { validateModelRoute } from './packages/web/server/lib/routing/model-routes.js';

validateModelRoute('r', { targets: [{ model: 'openai/gpt-6-luna', defaultVariant: '' }] });
// → null (accepted)
validateModelRoute('r', { targets: [{ model: 'openai/gpt-6-luna', variants: { low: '' } }] });
// → null (accepted)
validateModelRoute('r', { targets: [{ model: 'openai/gpt-6-luna', variants: { '': 'high' } }] });
// → null (accepted)
// control, valid settings → null (accepted, correct):
validateModelRoute('r', { targets: [{ model: 'openai/gpt-6-luna', defaultVariant: 'max', variants: { low: 'high' } }] });
```

OpenCode side (developing-today/opencode, current main):

- Schema (`packages/schema/src/config/model-routes.ts`, `TargetObject`): `defaultVariant` is a plain `Schema.String` and `variants` is `Schema.Record(Schema.String, Schema.String)`, so empty strings pass the schema — the validator is the only place they can be named. Real variant ids cannot be empty: `Model.VariantID` is checked against `/^[^#]+$/` (`packages/schema/src/config/model.ts`).
- Expansion (`packages/core/src/config/plugin/model-routes.ts`, `expand()`): the default variant is kept only when `spec.defaultVariant && model.variants.some((item) => item.id === spec.defaultVariant)` — `""` is falsy, so it is dropped without a word. The variant map is built by keeping only entries whose target value names a real variant of the model (`model.variants.some((item) => item.id === to)`) — `""` names none, so the remap is dropped; an empty route-level key can never be selected either, since a selected variant id is never `""`.

## Why it matters

An empty variant field is an easy artefact of a hand-edited or generated config, and of clearing a field in a JSON editor: the pool saves, the target shows the setting in the editor, and the model silently runs at its own default variant instead — the exact silent no-op this validator exists to prevent for every neighbouring field.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] Saving a target whose `defaultVariant` is an empty string is refused, with a message naming the route and saying the default variant must not be empty (or the field is otherwise judged exactly as OpenCode judges it — state the choice in the PR).
- [ ] Saving a target whose `variants` map has an empty key or an empty value is refused the same way.
- [ ] New cases are pinned in `packages/web/server/lib/routing/model-routes.test.js`: empty `defaultVariant` refused, empty variant value refused, empty variant key refused, and the existing valid target case (line ~147, `defaultVariant: 'max'`, `variants: { low: 'high' }`) still passes.
- [ ] The existing model-routes tests still pass.

## Problem

The model pool validator accepts a `weights` key that contains a `*` pattern, but OpenCode reads a route's weights only by the concrete key a target attaches with — a concrete leaf key (`provider/model`) or an `opencode-route/<id>` group key. A pattern key is never looked up, so the weight silently never applies and every expanded model keeps weight 1.

This is the weights arm of the exact defect #354 fixed for `budgets` (the validator now refuses a pattern budgets key with a dedicated message). The weights arm in `validateModelRoute` was not given the same guard.

## Evidence

Validator, `packages/web/server/lib/routing/model-routes.js` on current main (`94c06ab4e`):

- The weights loop (line ~346) accepts a key when `route.targets.some((target) => keyNamesTarget(key, target))`. `keyNamesTarget` returns true for an exact match — including when key and target are the same pattern — and for a sub-pattern of a pattern target.
- `validateBudgets` (line ~99) has the #354 guard: any key containing `*` is refused as "keyed by a pattern". No equivalent check exists for weights.

Direct probe against the real validator on current main:

```js
import { validateModelRoute } from './packages/web/server/lib/routing/model-routes.js';

validateModelRoute('r', { targets: ['openai/*'], weights: { 'openai/*': 3 } });
// → null (accepted — never applies)

validateModelRoute('r', { targets: ['openai/*'], weights: { 'openai/gpt-*': 3 } });
// → null (accepted — never applies)

// controls, both correctly accepted:
validateModelRoute('r', { targets: ['openai/*'], weights: { 'openai/gpt-6-luna': 3 } });
// → null (concrete key against a pattern target: OpenCode reads it by leaf key)
validateModelRoute('r', { targets: ['openai/gpt-6-luna'], weights: { 'openai/*': 3 } });
// → refused "matches no target" (already correct)
```

OpenCode side (developing-today/opencode, `packages/core/src/config/plugin/model-routes.ts`, `expand()`): each leaf is attached with `leafKey = ${providerID}/${id}` (line ~283) and each nested route with `opencode-route/<id>`; the weight is read as `current.weights?.[key]` with exactly that key (line ~251). A pattern target expands to concrete leaves, so a `weights` entry keyed `openai/*` or `openai/gpt-*` matches no attach key and is never read.

## Relation to existing issues

- #354 (closed) fixed this class for `budgets` keys only.
- #307 (open) is about the Pool health panel offering its Lower-weight action for pattern targets; this issue is the validator arm — a pattern-keyed weight saved through any path (hand-edited config, API, a future UI) is accepted by `validateModelRoute` today. Fixing the validator does not by itself close #307's panel criteria.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] `validateModelRoute` refuses any `weights` key containing `*`, with a message mirroring the budgets one (OpenCode reads weights by concrete target / route reference, so key the weight by a concrete target instead).
- [ ] Concrete weights keys against pattern targets, and `opencode-route/<id>` weights keys, still validate.
- [ ] A regression test in `model-routes.test.js` mirrors the budgets pattern test (exact pattern key and sub-pattern key refused).
- [ ] The routing DOCUMENTATION.md clause that lists refused shapes gains the pattern-weights case, mirroring the budgets clause.

## Problem

The model pool validator accepts a target `until` that can never apply, in two shapes, and saves both as silent no-ops:

1. **Already-expired `until` on a concrete target:** a target object whose `until` is at or before now validates and saves, but OpenCode's expansion skips that target outright (`Date.now() >= spec.until`), so the target leaves the route the moment the config is saved. If it is the route's only concrete target, the saved route expands to **zero models** — yet the validator's own expands-to-no-models check (#356) counts the expired target as concrete and lets the route through.
2. **`until` on a route-reference target:** a target object `{ model: "opencode-route/<id>", until: ... }` validates and saves, but OpenCode's expansion handles a route reference **before** it ever reads `until` — the referenced route is visited and attached unconditionally — so the `until` is silently ignored and the nested pool never leaves the route at that time.

This is the same saved-and-silently-ignored class as #354 / #363 / #369, applied to the one target field those fixes did not reach: `until` is currently checked only for being a positive whole number.

## Evidence

Validator, `packages/web/server/lib/routing/model-routes.js` on current main (`94c06ab4e`), `validateTarget` (lines ~279–280):

```js
if (target.until !== undefined && !(Number.isInteger(target.until) && target.until > 0))
  return "its until must be a positive epoch-millisecond time";
```

No comparison against the current time, and no distinction between concrete and route-reference targets.

Direct probe against the real validator on current main:

```js
import { validateModelRoute, validateModelRoutes } from './packages/web/server/lib/routing/model-routes.js';

// (1) expired sole target — accepted, OpenCode drops it, route expands to no models
validateModelRoutes({ r: { targets: [{ model: 'openai/gpt-6-luna', until: 1000 }] } });
// → null (accepted)
// (1b) expired target beside a live one — accepted, silently dropped
validateModelRoute('r', { targets: ['openai/gpt-6-luna', { model: 'openai/gpt-6-sol', until: 1000 }] });
// → null (accepted)
// (2) until on a route-reference target — accepted, OpenCode never reads it
validateModelRoutes({
  child:  { targets: ['openai/gpt-6-luna'] },
  parent: { targets: [{ model: 'opencode-route/child', until: 1000 }] },
});
// → null (accepted)
// control: a future until on a concrete target → null (accepted, correct)
// control: no until → null (accepted, correct)
```

OpenCode side (developing-today/opencode, current main):

- Expansion (`packages/core/src/config/plugin/model-routes.ts`, `expand()`): the route-reference branch runs first and `continue`s —

  ```ts
  if (ref.providerID === ModelRoute.PROVIDER_ID) {
    const child = visit(ref.id, ref.variant ?? inheritedVariant, nextTrail)
    if (child !== undefined) attach({ node: child }, `${ModelRoute.PROVIDER_ID}/${ref.id}`)
    continue
  }
  if (spec.until !== undefined && Date.now() >= spec.until) continue
  ```

  so `until` is only ever consulted for concrete targets, and there an at-or-past `until` drops the target without a word.
- Schema (`packages/schema/src/config/model-routes.ts`, `TargetObject.until`): "Epoch milliseconds after which this target leaves the route. Used for time-limited pool membership; takes effect when the configuration reloads." A past instant is therefore already-expired membership, not a future plan. (The validator only judges at save time, which is exactly when "already past" is decidable — a config whose `until` was future when saved expiring later is normal operation and out of scope.)

## Why it matters

`until` is how an operator time-limits pool membership (a trial provider, a quota window). Arm (1) lets a typo'd or stale timestamp — a seconds-instead-of-milliseconds value is the obvious one, `1000` vs `1000000000000` — silently remove a target, up to emptying the whole pool, while the editor still shows the target as a member. Arm (2) lets an operator believe a nested pool is time-limited when nothing will ever remove it. Both are exactly the misjudgements this validator exists to prevent, and arm (1) additionally defeats the expands-to-no-models guard added in #356.

## Acceptance criteria

- `validateModelRoute` / `validateModelRoutes` refuses a concrete target whose `until` is at or before the current time, with a message naming the route and saying the target's `until` is already past, so OpenCode would drop the target on save.
- A route whose concrete targets are **all** already-expired is refused (this follows from the first criterion at save time; pin it with a test so the expands-to-no-models guard cannot regress past it).
- An `until` on a target whose model is an `opencode-route/<id>` reference is refused, with a message saying OpenCode ignores `until` on route references (the nested route is included unconditionally), so the field would never apply. If runtime evidence shows a path where OpenCode does read it, pin that instead and close this arm as not-a-bug.
- Existing behaviour is unchanged otherwise: a future `until` on a concrete target still validates, non-integer / non-positive `until` values are still refused, and string targets are unaffected.
- Regression tests in `packages/web/server/lib/routing/model-routes.test.js` cover both arms plus the future-`until` control, and the existing routing tests still pass. (Tests should compute the past/future instants relative to `Date.now()` rather than hard-coding timestamps that age.)

Single-repo: the validator exists only in developing-today/openchamber (no `validateModelRoute` counterpart in developing-today/code, developing-today/opencode, or dezren39/nix — OpenCode is the runtime whose behaviour is cited, not a place a fix is needed). Filed-only: judging expiry is a one-spot check, but the route-reference arm needs the same direct-vs-reference distinction the set-level work in #363/#364 is introducing, so the sweeps should pick the ordering.

## Problem

The model pool validator accepts a schedule whose `when` window has already ended — a window `until` at or before now — and saves it, but OpenCode's schedule check can never return active for it again, so the schedule's weight change is a permanent silent no-op.

This is the same saved-but-never-applies class as #341 (equal start/end window) and #373 (already-past target `until`), applied to the one time bound those fixes did not reach: the schedule window's own `from`/`until` epoch bounds. `validateScheduleWindow` checks that `until` is a positive whole number and that `from` is before `until`, but never compares `until` against the current time.

## Evidence

Validator, `packages/web/server/lib/routing/model-routes.js` on current main (`94c06ab4e`), `validateScheduleWindow` (lines ~203–207):

```js
for (const key of ["from", "until"])
  if (window[key] !== undefined && !isWhole(window[key], 1, Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER))
    return `route "${id}" ${label} window.${key} must be a positive epoch-millisecond time`;
if (window.from !== undefined && window.until !== undefined && window.from >= window.until)
  return `route "${id}" ${label} window.from must be before window.until: OpenCode drops a window that can never apply`;
```

No comparison of `window.until` against now — even though the neighbouring check already uses the "can never apply" standard for `from >= until`.

Direct probe against the real validator on current main:

```js
import { validateModelRoute } from './packages/web/server/lib/routing/model-routes.js';

const base = { targets: ['openai/gpt-6-luna'] };
// window ended in 1970 — accepted, can never be active again
validateModelRoute('r', { ...base, schedules: [{ when: { until: 1000 }, factor: 2 }] });
// → null (accepted)
// wholly past window — accepted
validateModelRoute('r', { ...base, schedules: [{ when: { from: 1000, until: 2000 }, weight: 3 }] });
// → null (accepted)
// controls, correctly accepted:
validateModelRoute('r', { ...base, schedules: [{ when: { until: Date.now() + 86400000 }, factor: 2 }] });
// → null (accepted, correct)
validateModelRoute('r', { ...base, schedules: [{ when: { start: '09:00', end: '17:00' }, factor: 2 }] });
// → null (accepted, correct)
```

OpenCode side (developing-today/opencode, current main), `packages/core/src/model-route-schedule.ts`, `active()` (lines 268–270):

```ts
if (window.from !== undefined && now < window.from) return false
if (window.until !== undefined && now >= window.until) return false
```

Once `now >= window.until`, the window is inactive at every future moment — time does not run backwards — so the schedule it gates never applies, forever. The schema (`packages/schema/src/config/model-routes.ts`, `Window.until`) is only `PositiveInt`, so the validator is the only place this can be named.

## Why it matters

An ended window is an easy artefact of a copied or hand-edited config, or of a one-off schedule ("double the weight this weekend") that was never cleaned up: the pool saves, the schedule shows in the editor as if armed, and its weight change silently never happens again. That is the exact silent no-op this validator exists to prevent for every neighbouring field — it already refuses a window whose `from >= until` on precisely this ground.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] Saving a schedule whose `when.until` is at or before the current time is refused, with a message naming the route and schedule and saying the window has already ended / can never apply (judged at save time, like #373's target-`until` check).
- [ ] A window whose `until` is in the future still validates, as do windows with only `from`, only daily `start`/`end`, and no time bounds at all.
- [ ] New cases are pinned in `packages/web/server/lib/routing/model-routes.test.js`: past-`until` window refused (with and without `from`), future-`until` window accepted.
- [ ] The existing model-routes tests still pass.

## Problem

In the id web world page, clearing the **World seed** field silently installs/compiles the module with seed `0` instead of reporting an error — in both the install and the compile flows.

## Evidence (verified on main f7dfea35)

- The seed input is rendered with a preset value (`pkgs/id/src/web/templates.rs:793`):
  `<input data-world-seed ... type="number" value="1" aria-label="World seed">`
- Both handlers parse it the same way (`pkgs/id/web/src/world.ts:342-346` for install, `pkgs/id/web/src/world.ts:464-468` for compile):

  ```ts
  const seed = Number(moduleSeed.value);
  if (!Number.isSafeInteger(seed) || seed < 0) {
    setStatus("Seed must be a non-negative safe integer", "error");
    return;
  }
  ```

- `Number("") === 0` and `Number("  ") === 0` (verified with node), and `Number.isSafeInteger(0)` is `true` — so an emptied (or whitespace-only) field passes the guard and the request goes out with `seed: 0`.
- The seed is not cosmetic: the host starts the program from `init(seed)` (`pkgs/id/src/world.rs:583`), and the install seed is sensitive enough that join projections deliberately zero it (#240, `pkgs/id/src/world.rs:820-837`). Silently falling back to the predictable seed `0` when the user cleared the field (intending the preset `1`, or no seed decision at all) installs a world whose hidden state derives from a seed the admin never chose.

No existing issue covers this: search for seed-related issues returns #240 (seed redaction, closed), #556/#543/#534 (unrelated world features).

## Why it matters

- Silent wrong-value bug: the UI appears to validate the seed (it rejects negatives, fractions and unsafe integers) but misses the most common invalid state — an empty field.
- The same defect exists in both flows (install and compile), consistent with the duplicated parsing.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] Clearing the seed field and clicking Install shows "Seed must be a non-negative safe integer" (or an equivalent explicit empty-seed error) and sends no `install_begin` message.
- [ ] Same for the Compile flow: no `compile` message is sent with an empty seed.
- [ ] Valid seeds (including an explicit `0` typed by the user) still work in both flows.
- [ ] A regression test covers the empty-seed case (e.g. by extracting the parse into a shared, testable helper used by both handlers — which also removes the duplication).

## Suggested fix

Parse from the raw string and reject empty input before conversion, in one shared helper used by both handlers:

```ts
function parseSeed(raw: string): number | null {
  const trimmed = raw.trim();
  if (trimmed === "") return null;
  const seed = Number(trimmed);
  return Number.isSafeInteger(seed) && seed >= 0 ? seed : null;
}
```

## Problem

`isLightColor` in `pkgs/id/web/src/cursor-utils.ts:66-73` misclassifies CSS 3-digit shorthand hex colors as dark, no matter how light they are:

```ts
export function isLightColor(color: string): boolean {
  const hex = color.replace("#", "");
  const r = parseInt(hex.slice(0, 2), 16);
  const g = parseInt(hex.slice(2, 4), 16);
  const b = parseInt(hex.slice(4, 6), 16);
  const luminance = (0.299 * r + 0.587 * g + 0.114 * b) / 255;
  return luminance > 0.5;
}
```

For a 3-digit input the slices misalign: `"fff"` → r=`"ff"`=255, g=`"f"`=15, b=`""`=`NaN`, so the luminance is `NaN` and `NaN > 0.5` is `false`.

## Verified (verbatim function, node, on main 4f66baae)

- `isLightColor("#fff")` → `false` (white — expected `true`)
- `isLightColor("#ff0")` → `false` (yellow — expected `true`)
- `isLightColor("#000")` → `false` (correct only by accident)
- Any non-hex input (e.g. `"red"`, `"#gggggg"`, `""`) also silently yields `NaN` → `false`, with no distinction from a genuinely dark color.

## Why it matters

`isLightColor` picks the label text color for remote cursors at `pkgs/id/web/src/cursors.ts:352` and `cursors.ts:431` (`isLightColor(cursor.color) ? "#000" : "#fff"`). A cursor color arrives from peers/server via cursor updates (`cursors.ts:826`, `color: cursorUpdate.color`) and is never validated to be 6-digit hex. A peer whose color is shorthand white/yellow gets white text on a white/yellow label background — the name label is illegible. The existing tests (`cursor-utils.test.ts:162-203`) cover only 6-digit forms (with and without `#`), so the gap is invisible to CI.

## Acceptance criteria

- `isLightColor("#fff") === true`, `isLightColor("#ff0") === true`, `isLightColor("#000") === false` — 3-digit shorthand is expanded per CSS rules (each digit doubled) before parsing.
- Malformed/non-hex input has a defined, tested behavior (e.g. treated as dark) instead of falling out of a `NaN` comparison by accident.
- New cases added to `cursor-utils.test.ts`; the existing cursor-utils suite still passes.
- Single-repo: the only copy of this helper is in developing-today/code `pkgs/id/web` (checked the other three repos' clones — no other `isLightColor`).

## Problem

`needsRefresh()` in `pkgs/id/web/src/main.ts:291-296` silently suppresses identity-token refresh forever when the stored refresh timestamp is not numeric:

```ts
function needsRefresh(): boolean {
  const refreshedAt = localStorage.getItem(IDENTITY_REFRESHED_AT_KEY);
  if (!refreshedAt) return true;
  const elapsed = Date.now() - Number(refreshedAt);
  return elapsed >= IDENTITY_REFRESH_INTERVAL_MS;
}
```

`Number("garbage")` is `NaN`; `Date.now() - NaN` is `NaN`; `NaN >= interval` is `false`. So any non-numeric value in `id_identity_refreshed_at` (corrupt storage, a value written by another build/version, manual tampering) makes `needsRefresh()` return `false` on every SPA navigation, indefinitely — the exact path (`main.ts`, `onMainSwapped`) whose comment says it "keeps the 30-day token alive" never calls `ensureIdentity()`.

Verified with node on main 4f66baae: `Date.now() - Number("garbage") >= 86400000` → `false`. A future/garbage-but-numeric timestamp has the same shape: a far-future value yields a negative elapsed and also suppresses refresh until wall-clock time catches up.

## Why it matters

The failure mode is silent and total for the affected browser profile: the token is never proactively refreshed, so the user is eventually logged out at the 30-day expiry despite using the app daily via SPA navigation. `localStorage` is untrusted, user-writable state — parsing it with a bare `Number()` and no finiteness check is the same silent-reinterpretation family the id web code has been fixing elsewhere (#707/#832 world-seed, #712 goto-line), but here the bad value disables a keep-alive instead of producing a visible error.

## Acceptance criteria

- A non-numeric or non-finite stored timestamp is treated as "needs refresh" (`true`), as is a timestamp implausibly far in the future.
- Normal behavior unchanged: missing key → `true`; elapsed ≥ 24h interval → `true`; recent timestamp → `false`.
- The check is covered by a test (extracting the parse into a small testable helper next to the other `pkgs/id/web/src/*.test.ts` suites is fine), or an equivalent verified repro is attached to the fixing PR.
- Single-repo: `needsRefresh`/`IDENTITY_REFRESHED_AT_KEY` exist only in developing-today/code `pkgs/id/web/src/main.ts`.

## Finding

`QueueClient.ensureWorker()` in pm-dispatch memoizes its registration promise with `??=` — including when that promise **rejects**. One transient registration failure (a PostgREST 5xx, a network blip) therefore wedges every subsequent launch for the life of the process: each later `ensureWorker()` returns the same rejected promise and throws the original, stale error.

## Evidence

- PR #2655 (commit `88e2e699e1`, MERGED) introduced `ensureWorker()`.
- Verified on current main (`c4fb599617166d4c705eabeb34442ca179ecb9a6`), `pm-stack/dispatch/src/queue.ts:132,137-158`:

  ```ts
  private workerEnsured: Promise<void> | null = null;

  ensureWorker(): Promise<void> {
    this.workerEnsured ??= (async () => {
      const res = await fetch(`${this.baseUrl}/rest/v1/workers`, { … });
      if (!res.ok) {
        const text = await res.text();
        throw new QueueError(`worker registration failed (HTTP ${res.status}): …`, res.status);
      }
    })();
    return this.workerEnsured;
  }
  ```

  There is no `.catch` that resets `workerEnsured` to `null`, and no other code path clears it.
- Caller order makes it worse: `launchSession` (`src/server.ts:116-129`) creates the OpenCode session and sends the prompt **before** `ensureWorker()` runs. So every launch after the first failure creates a real, running session, then throws at registration — and on the `wait:true` path the task is cancelled and the launch reported FAILED while that session keeps working, untracked.

## Why it matters

Registration is a one-time, idempotent upsert whose failure modes are overwhelmingly transient — this exact class of failure is what the fleet hit repeatedly on 2026-10-30 (PostgREST 503s during the Supabase outage). A retry-on-next-launch costs nothing; a permanently poisoned cache turns a blip into a dispatch outage that only a service restart fixes, plus a trail of untracked sessions.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] A rejected registration attempt is not cached: `workerEnsured` is reset on rejection so the next `ensureWorker()` call retries the upsert (a *successful* registration stays cached).
- [ ] A unit test covers: first attempt fails (non-OK response or fetch throw), second attempt succeeds, and subsequent calls reuse the success without re-fetching.
- [ ] No other behaviour of `ensureWorker()` changes (same worker row, same ignore-duplicates upsert).

Found by the pr-diff-review job reviewing PR #2655 (batch 2630–2658, 2026-10-30). Deduped: `gh issue list --search 'ensureWorker worker registration dispatch'` and `'workerEnsured'` (open + all) returned nothing covering this.
